  "title": "Value Tata Motors via Sum-of-the-Parts and Spot the Holding Company Discount",
  "scenario": "You are a Mumbai equity analyst tasked with valuing Tata Motors—a sprawling conglomerate with Jaguar Land Rover (JLR), India commercial vehicles, India passenger vehicles, and a finance arm—using sum-of-the-parts (SOTP). Your desk also monitors Bajaj Holdings, a pure holding company trading at a steep discount to NAV. You'll value each segment by its own method, compute the SOTP equity value per share, and measure the holding company discount to understand why trapped value stays trapped.",
  "instructions": "Build a SOTP valuation for Tata Motors by assigning each segment its appropriate multiple and valuing enterprise value; then compute per-share SOTP equity value after subtracting net debt and finance P/B adjustments. For Bajaj Holdings, calculate NAV per share and the holding company discount from market price. Answers sheet has 5 cells; formulas must reference the Data sheet.",
  "learning_objective": "Compute sum-of-the-parts equity value for a multi-segment conglomerate, value each business by its own appropriate method, and quantify the holding company discount to understand why discounts persist and when catalysts unlock value.",
  "hints": [
    "Start by summing the enterprise values of the four auto segments (JLR, CV, PV, EV platform) using SUM, then subtract net debt to get operating equity value.",
    "For Tata Motors Finance, use its Price-to-Book ratio (not EBITDA) to value it separately; for Bajaj Holdings, calculate NAV as the sum of all stakeholder value divided by shares outstanding.",
    "The holding company discount is (NAV − Market Price) / NAV. If it's 50%, the market pays ₹50 for every ₹100 of assets. Check your Bajaj Holdings discount against the provided market price."
  ],
